  "ai_language_tip": "Dzongkha (dz) is the national language but Bageo cannot speak it; English is the school language, so English works with almost every business in Thimphu and Paro.",
  "english_ok": "yes — Schooling is in English; offices, hotels, clinics and tour operators all handle it; older villagers may rely on Dzongkha or Nepali.",
  "politeness": "Respectful and gentle; add 'la' at the end of replies as Bhutanese do, address men as Dasho only if titled, otherwise Aum (Ms) or Ap (Mr).",
  "name_tip": "Most Bhutanese have two given names and no family name, so book under both names exactly as said; many names are shared by men and women.",
  "phone_tip": "Mobiles are 8 digits starting 17 or 16 (B-Mobile) or 77 (TashiCell); landlines are 7 digits with a regional 0 code; read in 2+3+3.",
  "time_tip": "12-hour in speech, 24-hour on tickets; confirm morning or evening and remember Bhutan is UTC+6.",
  "number_tip": "Prices are in ngultrum (Nu), pegged one-to-one with the Indian rupee; many shops also take rupees.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid lunch 13:00–14:00 and calls after 19:00; Tsechu festival days and national holidays close many offices.",
  "closed_days": "Government offices close Saturday and Sunday; shops open most days; Tuesday is a dry day when bars stop selling alcohol.",
  "booking_channels": "WeChat and WhatsApp carry much business chat; foreign visitors' trips are arranged through licensed tour operators; payments via mobile banking apps such as mBoB.",
  "voice_menu": "Bhutan Telecom and TashiCell have short IVR menus; small shops answer directly.",
  "callback_habits": "Shops reply on WeChat or WhatsApp; tour operators confirm by email; clinics usually call back.",
